{info:title=Database Dynamic Key Rotation with HashiCorp Vault}
Implement dynamic database credential generation using Vault's Database secrets engine to eliminate static passwords and enhance security.
{info}

{panel:title=Page Information|borderStyle=dashed|borderColor=#ccc|titleBGColor=#d9ead3|bgColor=#f9f9f9}
*Target Audience:* Database Administrators, DevOps Engineers, Developers
*Prerequisites:* Database admin access, Vault cluster, network connectivity
*Related Pages:* [Keystore Management|03-keystore-management-guide], [Azure Dynamic Keys|04-azure-dynamic-key-rotation]
{panel}

h1. Overview

Database dynamic key rotation generates temporary database credentials on-demand, eliminating static passwords and reducing security risks through automated credential lifecycle management.

{panel:title=Key Benefits|borderStyle=solid|borderColor=#2c3e50|titleBGColor=#34495e|titleColor=#ffffff}
• Short-lived credentials (minutes to hours)
• Automatic cleanup when expired
• Role-based database access control
• Zero static secrets in applications
• Complete audit trail
{panel}

h1. Supported Databases

{panel:title=Supported Database Types|borderStyle=solid|borderColor=#27ae60|titleBGColor=#2ecc71|titleColor=#ffffff}
*SQL:* PostgreSQL, MySQL/MariaDB, SQL Server, Oracle
*NoSQL:* MongoDB, Cassandra, Redis, Elasticsearch
*Others:* InfluxDB, CouchDB, HanaDB
{panel}

h1. Setup

h2. Enable Database Engine

{code:language=bash|title=Enable Database Secrets Engine}
# Enable database secrets engine
vault secrets enable database

# Verify enabled
vault secrets list
{code}

h2. PostgreSQL Configuration

{code:language=bash|title=PostgreSQL Setup}
# Configure connection
vault write database/config/postgresql \
    plugin_name=postgresql-database-plugin \
    connection_url="postgresql://{{username}}:{{password}}@postgres.example.com:5432/mydb?sslmode=require" \
    allowed_roles="readonly,readwrite" \
    username="vault_admin" \
    password="admin_password"

# Create read-only role
vault write database/roles/readonly \
    db_name=postgresql \
    creation_statements="CREATE ROLE \"{{name}}\" WITH LOGIN PASSWORD '{{password}}' VALID UNTIL '{{expiration}}'; GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO \"{{name}}\";" \
    default_ttl="1h" \
    max_ttl="24h"

# Create read-write role
vault write database/roles/readwrite \
    db_name=postgresql \
    creation_statements="CREATE ROLE \"{{name}}\" WITH LOGIN PASSWORD '{{password}}' VALID UNTIL '{{expiration}}'; GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO \"{{name}}\";" \
    revocation_statements="DROP ROLE IF EXISTS \"{{name}}\";" \
    default_ttl="2h" \
    max_ttl="8h"
{code}

h2. MySQL Configuration

{code:language=bash|title=MySQL Setup}
# Configure connection
vault write database/config/mysql \
    plugin_name=mysql-database-plugin \
    connection_url="{{username}}:{{password}}@tcp(mysql.example.com:3306)/" \
    allowed_roles="app,readonly" \
    username="vault_admin" \
    password="admin_password"

# Create application role
vault write database/roles/app \
    db_name=mysql \
    creation_statements="CREATE USER '{{name}}'@'%' IDENTIFIED BY '{{password}}'; GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON myapp.* TO '{{name}}'@'%';" \
    revocation_statements="DROP USER '{{name}}'@'%';" \
    default_ttl="1h" \
    max_ttl="4h"
{code}

h2. MongoDB Configuration

{code:language=bash|title=MongoDB Setup}
# Configure connection
vault write database/config/mongodb \
    plugin_name=mongodb-database-plugin \
    connection_url="mongodb://{{username}}:{{password}}@mongo.example.com:27017/admin" \
    allowed_roles="app,readonly" \
    username="vault_admin" \
    password="admin_password"

# Create application role
vault write database/roles/app \
    db_name=mongodb \
    creation_statements='{"db": "myapp", "roles": [{"role": "readWrite", "db": "myapp"}]}' \
    default_ttl="2h" \
    max_ttl="6h"
{code}

h1. Credential Management

h2. Generate Credentials

{code:language=bash|title=Generate Database Credentials}
# Generate credentials
vault read database/creds/readonly

# Example output:
# lease_id           database/creds/readonly/abc123
# lease_duration     3600
# password           A1a-randompassword123
# username           v-token-readonly-xyz789

# Store in variables
DB_CREDS=$(vault read -format=json database/creds/readonly)
LEASE_ID=$(echo $DB_CREDS | jq -r '.lease_id')
USERNAME=$(echo $DB_CREDS | jq -r '.data.username')
PASSWORD=$(echo $DB_CREDS | jq -r '.data.password')
{code}

h2. Lease Management

{code:language=bash|title=Manage Credential Leases}
# Renew lease
vault lease renew $LEASE_ID

# Renew with specific duration
vault lease renew -increment=3600 $LEASE_ID

# Revoke lease
vault lease revoke $LEASE_ID

# List active leases
vault list sys/leases/lookup/database/creds/readonly
{code}

h1. Application Integration

h2. Python Example

{code:language=python|title=Python Integration}
import hvac
import psycopg2
from contextlib import contextmanager

class DatabaseVaultClient:
    def __init__(self, vault_url, vault_token):
        self.vault_client = hvac.Client(url=vault_url, token=vault_token)
        
    def get_credentials(self, role_name):
        response = self.vault_client.read(f'database/creds/{role_name}')
        return {
            'username': response['data']['username'],
            'password': response['data']['password'],
            'lease_id': response['lease_id']
        }
    
    @contextmanager
    def get_postgres_connection(self, role_name, host, database):
        creds = self.get_credentials(role_name)
        connection = None
        try:
            connection = psycopg2.connect(
                host=host, database=database,
                user=creds['username'], password=creds['password']
            )
            yield connection
        finally:
            if connection:
                connection.close()
            self.vault_client.sys.revoke_lease(creds['lease_id'])

# Usage
vault_client = DatabaseVaultClient(
    "https://vault.example.com:8200", "hvs.token"
)

with vault_client.get_postgres_connection('readonly', 'db.example.com', 'myapp') as conn:
    cursor = conn.cursor()
    cursor.execute("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users")
    print(f"Users: {cursor.fetchone()[0]}")
{code}

h2. Shell Script Example

{code:language=bash|title=Bash Integration}
#!/bin/bash
# Get credentials and connect to database

ROLE_NAME="readonly"
DB_HOST="postgres.example.com"

# Get credentials from Vault
CREDS=$(vault read -format=json database/creds/$ROLE_NAME)
USERNAME=$(echo $CREDS | jq -r '.data.username')
PASSWORD=$(echo $CREDS | jq -r '.data.password')
LEASE_ID=$(echo $CREDS | jq -r '.lease_id')

# Cleanup function
cleanup() {
    vault lease revoke $LEASE_ID
}
trap cleanup EXIT

# Connect to database
export PGHOST=$DB_HOST PGUSER=$USERNAME PGPASSWORD=$PASSWORD
psql -d myapp -c "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users"
{code}

h1. Best Practices

{panel:title=Security & Performance|borderStyle=solid|borderColor=#e67e22|titleBGColor=#f39c12|titleColor=#ffffff}
*Security:*
• Use principle of least privilege for roles
• Enable comprehensive audit logging
• Restrict database access by network
• Regular role and policy reviews

*Performance:*
• Cache credentials with TTL handling
• Renew at 2/3 of lease duration
• Use connection pooling
• Monitor credential generation rates
{panel}

h1. Troubleshooting

{expand:title=Common Issues}
*Credential Generation Fails:*
• Test connectivity: {{vault write database/config/db/verify-connection}}
• Check admin user permissions in database
• Verify connection string format

*Authentication Failures:*
• Check credential TTL and timing
• Wait for user propagation (30 seconds)
• Verify network connectivity
• Review database authentication logs
{expand}

